| RunReadtest2 - how read() and fread() behave when the destination memory has never been touched.  Every case is its own program run, so its stack is fresh.
| Output: ResultsReadtest2 in this directory.  (the first EABI program run changes <Obey$Dir>, so remember our directory first)
Set RoTest$Dir <Obey$Dir>
Dir <RoTest$Dir>
Spool <RoTest$Dir>.ResultsReadtest2
Echo ===================== what a missing pointer advance in fread() would look like (a simulation, nothing is read from the file system)
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 model 64
Echo ===================== read() into a fresh stack buffer
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 read 8
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 read 16
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 read 32
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 read 64
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 read 128
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 read 256
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 read 512
Echo ===================== fread() into a fresh stack buffer
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 fread 8
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 fread 16
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 fread 32
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 fread 64
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 fread 128
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 fread 256
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 fread 512
Echo ===================== fread() into a stack buffer that was written first
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 touch 64
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 touch 512
Echo ===================== fresh mmap and malloc memory
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 mmap 64
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 mmap 512
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 malloc 64
Run <RoTest$Dir>.readtest2 malloc 1024
Spool
Echo Finished. Output is in <RoTest$Dir>.ResultsReadtest2
